;;; `std.algebra/transitive` — R;R ⊆ R, and the `(neq ?a ?c)` guard that is
;;; the only difference between it and `(compose R R R)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the rule promises ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   (transitive R)   R(a,b) ∧ R(b,c) ∧ a ≠ c ⟹ R(a,c)
;;;
;;; The module's own description is "the guarded special case of
;;; `(compose R R R)` — the guard suppresses the self-loop a bare
;;; compose-into-self would add, which keeps an irreflexive closure
;;; irreflexive". That sentence is a claim about two rules, so this file runs
;;; both, over the same two-cycle, and the answers differ by exactly the
;;; self-loops.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to them ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A two-cycle is the smallest shape on which the guard bites: A→B and B→A.
;;;
;;;   tr   (transitive tr)      A→B→A would close to A→A — `neq` refuses.
;;;                             B→A→B likewise. So `tr` never grows, and the
;;;                             closure of a cycle stays irreflexive.
;;;   cyc  (compose cyc cyc cyc)  the same two edges with no guard: both
;;;                             self-loops appear.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `tr` closed at two edges is the guard's test in the direction that finds a
;;; bug: drop the `(neq ?a ?c)` and `(tr A A)` appears as a surplus fact. `cyc`
;;; closed at four is the control — it says the two rules were both reached and
;;; that the difference is the guard rather than the fixture.
;;;
;;; So `transitive` **fires zero times here**, and that is the point rather
;;; than a gap: every match it finds is one the guard refuses. A rule that is
;;; only ever tested where it fires has had its guard tested nowhere.
;;;
;;; `transitive` fires in six corpus entries already, all of them puzzles whose
;;; hierarchies are acyclic, where the guard can never be the thing that
;;; decides. Here it is the only thing that decides.

(import std.algebra :symbols (transitive compose))

(relation tr  Node Node :why "{?1} reaches {?2}")
(relation cyc Node Node)

(transitive tr)
(compose    cyc cyc cyc)

(tr  A B) (tr  B A)
(cyc A B) (cyc B A)

(query
  :goal (tr ?x ?y)
  :no-hypothesis (tr cyc)
  :expect (model (tr  A B) (tr  B A)
                 (cyc A B) (cyc B A) (cyc A A) (cyc B B)))
